WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
"The latest in school segregation"

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:06.880
was how the headline of a recent New York Times op-ed described private "pandemic pods,"

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.140
in which parents get together and hire an in-person teacher

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.180
while public schools remain remote.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.060
The pandemic pods, says the writer,

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.920
"will exacerbate inequities, racial segregation and the opportunity gap within schools."

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.980
Business Insider had a slightly different take,

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.300
they said the pods were "inequitable and inevitable"

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.400
and that the arrangements were "a dream come true for the school choice movement."

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.120
But are pandemic pods just the latest tool through which white parents use their financial and political clout

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.320
to separate out their kids, thus increasing educational segregation?

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.280
And is the solution to increase government spending in K-12 schools

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.080
so that all parents will want to keep their kids in the public system?

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.380
That was the subject of an online Soho Forum debate held on Wednesday, September 16th, 2020.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.200
Arguing for more government spending was Jon Hale,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.340
a professor of education at the University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.100
Arguing in favor of pods and other parental innovations was Corey DeAngelis,

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.160
the director of school choice at the Reason Foundation,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.980
which publishes ReasonTV.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:16.160
Here's Jon Hale and Corey DeAngelis in an online debate moderated by the Soho Forum's Director Gene Epstein.

